/*
Problem: Binary Tree Postorder Traversal (LC 145)
Topic: Binary Tree / DFS

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Postorder traversal visits nodes in
the following order:

Left -> Right -> Root

Recursively:

1. Traverse the left subtree.

2. Traverse the right subtree.

3. Visit the current node.

Store every visited node in the answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

For every node:

• If node is NULL,
  return.

• Traverse left subtree.

• Traverse right subtree.

• Store current node value.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

        1
         \
          2
         /
        3

Traversal:

Left of 1

↓

Left of 2

↓

Visit 3

↓

Visit 2

↓

Visit 1

Answer:

[3,2,1]

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The recursive calls ensure that both
subtrees are completely processed
before the current node is visited.

Thus the order is always:

Left -> Right -> Root

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Every node is visited exactly once.

Space : O(h)

h = height of tree

(O(log n) for balanced,
 O(n) for skewed tree)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Tree DFS (Postorder Traversal)
*/

class Solution {
public:

    void postOrder(TreeNode* root,
                   vector<int>& ans) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return;

        // Left subtree
        postOrder(root->left, ans);

        // Right subtree
        postOrder(root->right, ans);

        // Visit current node
        ans.push_back(root->val);
    }

    vector<int> postorderTraversal(TreeNode* root) {

        vector<int> ans;

        postOrder(root, ans);

        return ans;
    }
};
